# <%= projectName %>

Go walking skeleton scaffolded by keel — hexagonal, one module, no
mediator object: commands are structs, driving ports are per-use-case
interfaces, and each `cmd/` main is the assembly point.

Carved on the **modulith** layout: one bounded context per directory
under `internal/modules/`, each a whole hexagon.

## Layout

```
cmd/                    — one directory per deployment unit; each
                          main.go wires the hexagon by hand
internal/
  platform/             — what no bounded context owns: the Clock
                          port and its adapters, correlation ids
  modules/
    greeting/           — one bounded context
      greeting.go       — THE FACADE: factories only, no type
                          aliases. A consumer holds what this
                          returns; it cannot name it
      internal/
        domain/         — the contract face: commands, ports, and
                          the factories the facade calls
          internal/     — the compiler-hidden core
      userside/         — primary adapters: transport → command →
                          port → transport; zero business logic
        service/        — THE PEER SEAM: what another context may
                          import, in types it may write down
      infra/            — secondary adapters, one per technology
```

Three placements are load-bearing, and Go's compiler enforces each:

- **`internal/` hides the context's core.** Anything under
  `modules/greeting/internal/` is importable from inside
  `modules/greeting/` and from nowhere else.
- **Adapters sit beside that wall, not behind it.** `userside/` and
  `infra/` are inside the context (so they may name its ports) but
  outside its `internal/` — otherwise `cmd/` could not construct
  them.
- **The facade re-exports nothing.** No `type Greeter = …`, no
  `var ErrEmptyName = …`. That is what makes "only this context may
  implement its own ports" a compile error rather than a review
  comment, and it costs the assembly nothing: `:=` infers the type
  it may not write.

A second context is a sibling directory under `internal/modules/`,
and it reaches this one through `userside/service` — the peer seam.

That seam is here for a different reason than the JVM's or Rust's.
There it _narrows_ what a peer may reach. Go has no such lever:
`internal/` is scoped to the project root, so every package under it
may import every other and the seam narrows nothing.

What Go enforces is where the domain sits. It is behind
`modules/greeting/internal/`, so a peer importing it fails to build
(`use of internal package … not allowed`) — greeting's commands,
ports and error values are unreachable from any other context, as a
compile error rather than a review comment. A peer can still reach
the facade, and cannot name one thing it returns.

That last part is worth stating precisely, because the stronger
version is false: unnameability does not stop a peer calling through.
Go's assignability is structural for unnamed types, so a foreign
package can write `greeting.NewGreeter().Greet(struct{ Name string }
{…})` and it compiles. What it gets is coupling nothing declares, to
a shape that breaks the first time this context adds a field — which
is the argument for using the seam, not a hole in it. `userside/
service` declares the types a peer may write down, and they are this
context's own: what crosses is a `service.Greeting` and a
`service.Unavailable`, never a domain type or a domain error.

Note that two contexts can declare the same package name
(`modules/ordering` and `modules/billing/gateway/ordering` are both
`package ordering`, and every context's seam is `package service`),
so a file importing both must alias one.

Cross-cutting concerns (logging, metrics, tx) wrap the driving ports
as decorator functions applied at the assembly point.

## Test

```sh
go test ./...
```

## Deployment units

Each `cmd/` directory is one deployment unit; its `main.go` import
list reads like the ops runbook.
